{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And Esau saw that he had blessed, etc.</b> This verse has no continuation, and if its continuation is ״וַיֵּלֶךְ עֵשָׂו אֶל יִשְׁמָעֵאל״ (Genesis 28:9), it would have been sufficient with the verse after this: ״וַיַּרְא עֵשָׂו כִּי רָעוֹת״ (Genesis 28:8)." "<b>Indeed,</b> the intent of the verse is to inform us that Esau knew of Jacob's departure and its direction — that he went in obedience to his father and mother and also in fulfillment of the commandment to take a wife, and also that they blessed him when they sent him off. For on this account he did not pursue him, for he knew that he would be withheld from his hand, since he was going for two commandments: honoring father and mother, and taking a wife. And although he sent Eliphaz, as they of blessed memory said (Genesis 29:11), it is possible that he considered that he would encounter Jacob on the road, and ״the deeds of the fathers are a sign for the sons״ — which he anticipated on the road for Israel — and Esau reckoned that the power of Eliphaz was greater than his own power in the aspect of pursuit on the road against Jacob and his descendants."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {0 "Reading גִּזְרָה as 'continuation/sequence' (from גזרת, the cut/following portion); the logical difficulty is that verse 8 appears to follow naturally from verse 6, making verse 6 itself seem superfluous as an independent statement.", 1 "The verse citation (Genesis 29:11) appears to refer to the rabbinic midrash about Eliphaz pursuing Jacob; the citation address may be displaced or refer to a different textual witness."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:44:13.325824791Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}